# Access and Security Checklist

Use this checklist before connecting the workflow to real devices, systems, or operational data.

## Asset and device identity

- [ ] Every device or asset has a unique identifier.
- [ ] The physical identifier matches the system record.
- [ ] Duplicate identifiers are prevented.
- [ ] Unknown devices and assets are flagged for review.
- [ ] Asset ownership and location are recorded.

## User access

- [ ] Users authenticate before viewing operational data.
- [ ] Access is based on role and responsibility.
- [ ] Technicians can access only the assets they need.
- [ ] Administrative actions require additional authorisation.
- [ ] Former users lose access promptly.
- [ ] Shared user accounts are avoided.

## Data protection

- [ ] Sensitive data is protected during transmission.
- [ ] Sensitive data is protected when stored.
- [ ] Credentials and secrets are not stored in CSV files.
- [ ] Personal information is collected only when necessary.
- [ ] Logs do not expose passwords, access tokens, or private keys.
- [ ] Data retention and deletion rules are defined.

## Device and system changes

- [ ] Device configuration changes require authorisation.
- [ ] Software updates use an approved process.
- [ ] Changes are recorded in an audit log.
- [ ] Production changes are tested before release.
- [ ] Emergency changes have a review process.

## Alert handling

- [ ] Critical alerts have a named responsible team.
- [ ] Alert ownership is visible.
- [ ] Duplicate alerts can be identified.
- [ ] Alert closure requires appropriate approval.
- [ ] Escalation conditions are documented.
- [ ] The system records who reviewed and closed an alert.

## AI usage

- [ ] AI output is treated as a recommendation.
- [ ] Human review is required before important action.
- [ ] The AI is not allowed to change device settings by default.
- [ ] The AI is not allowed to close critical alerts automatically.
- [ ] Prompts and outputs are reviewed for incorrect assumptions.
- [ ] Approved data sources and AI tools are defined.

## Before production use

- [ ] Security testing is complete.
- [ ] Failure and outage scenarios have been tested.
- [ ] Monitoring and audit logging are enabled.
- [ ] A rollback process exists.
- [ ] The responsible owner has approved the workflow.
